WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.916
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:10.370
TOM MELENDEZ: Hi, I'm Tom, a
software engineer at Google.

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:12.740
This is the first video in
our series Using Ansible

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:14.780
with Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:17.420
We can use many available
Ansible models to manage

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:19.240
the various services in GCP.

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:20.750
Dylan is here from
Ansible to tell

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:22.580
us more about how
a DevOps team can

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:25.175
take advantage of these features
available in the engine.

00:00:25.175 --> 00:00:27.050
DYLAN SILVA: Ansible is
a YAML-based language

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:30.080
that can perfectly describe
entire infrastructures

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:31.580
in Ansible playbooks.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:34.460
It is also the engine that
executes these playbooks.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:39.170
This execution is performed over
SSH or WinRM without an agent.

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:42.200
Currently, there are
over 1,000 plus modules

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:43.687
that enable these tasks.

00:00:43.687 --> 00:00:45.770
TOM MELENDEZ: And the
amount of user contributions

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:47.400
is impressive.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.510
DYLAN SILVA: Yeah, we have
over 2,500 unique contributors

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:53.780
at this current time,
over 22,000 stars,

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:56.360
and 7,000 plus forks on GitHub.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.150
We even have Ansible books
written by community members

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:00.290
as well.

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:01.850
Over the years,
we've seen Ansible

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:03.380
do some pretty unique things.

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:09.022
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:09.022 --> 00:01:10.730
TOM MELENDEZ: In these
next few episodes,

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:13.070
we're going to talk about
how we used Ansible to manage

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:14.870
a satellite imaging system.

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:17.670
We can even send commands to
the satellite via our Ansible

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:18.350
playbook.

00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:20.610
Ansible is quite
a powerful tool.

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:23.000
In this video series, we'll
talk about how you can use it

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.790
in launching instances,
deploying across region load

00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:28.640
balancer, and configuring
customized networks.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:31.082
We'll even discuss how to
build a custom network.

00:01:31.082 --> 00:01:32.040
DYLAN SILVA: All right.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:35.510
So let's talk a little bit more
about that Ansible controlling

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:37.550
a satellite thing you've
got going on there.

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:38.341
TOM MELENDEZ: Sure.

00:01:38.341 --> 00:01:39.980
The satellite imaging
system consists

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:43.430
of three subsystems,
commanding, image processing,

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:45.540
and end-user applications.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:49.100
DYLAN SILVA: So commanding,
you mean Ansible is actually

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:51.680
working to control
the actual satellite

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:54.140
and have the satellite
do some functions, right?

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:54.890
TOM MELENDEZ: Yes.

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:56.960
As satellites orbit
the Earth, they

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.420
go in and out of remote
ground station coverage.

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:01.730
It's ideal to place ground
stations near the poles

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:03.230
so they will have
more opportunities

00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:05.300
to communicate with satellites.

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:08.509
In our series, we use Ansible
to launch some instances,

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:11.870
run an executable on
some camera parameters,

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:13.550
and then call a
custom module to send

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:15.434
those parameters to the RGS.

00:02:15.434 --> 00:02:16.100
DYLAN SILVA: OK.

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:17.901
So tell me about
image processing now.

00:02:17.901 --> 00:02:19.400
TOM MELENDEZ: Well,
image processing

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:20.780
is a sensitive issue.

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:23.330
Basically, images that come
directly from the satellite

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:25.190
are not ready for human use.

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:27.950
There is a custom processing
that needs to occur.

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:30.500
In this series, we talk about
building custom networks

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:33.410
for both the image processing
system and the remote ground

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:34.550
station.

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:36.320
DYLAN SILVA: And
end-user applications

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:38.430
allow the user to see
the processed images?

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:39.680
TOM MELENDEZ: Correct.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:41.346
DYLAN SILVA: I would
expect these images

00:02:41.346 --> 00:02:43.220
to be quite large, right?

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:45.770
And you probably have
customers all over the world.

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:48.550
How do you keep the
response time low

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:51.181
and the experience good?

00:02:51.181 --> 00:02:53.180
TOM MELENDEZ: Well, we
use the cross region load

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:56.720
balancer functionality built
in to GCP to ensure users

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:59.449
are getting directed to
the closest data center.

00:02:59.449 --> 00:03:01.490
We'll also cover how to
set this up with Ansible.

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:02.810
DYLAN SILVA: Very cool.

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:04.790
And you mentioned
the custom module.

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:07.280
We at Ansible take a
lot of pride in fact

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:10.556
that Ansible is extensible
enough for you to do that.

00:03:10.556 --> 00:03:11.430
TOM MELENDEZ: Agreed.

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:13.160
To update our remote
ground station,

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:14.720
we built a custom module.

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:16.430
We can't go over that
module in detail,

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:18.610
but we can show the
audience how to build

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:20.780
their own modules for
either internal use

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:21.742
or to be open sourced.

00:03:21.742 --> 00:03:23.450
DYLAN SILVA: So that
being said, let's be

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:28.040
sure to cover documentation and
some best practices, too, OK?

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:30.860
We want to hold community-built
modules to the same standards

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:32.670
as our own delivered modules.

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:33.545
TOM MELENDEZ: Got it.

00:03:33.545 --> 00:03:35.919
DYLAN SILVA: I'm looking
forward to talking about Ansible

00:03:35.919 --> 00:03:37.280
and Google Cloud together, Tom.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:38.155
TOM MELENDEZ: Me too.

00:03:38.155 --> 00:03:40.760
And don't forget to check out
all of our Ansible modules

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:42.590
at the URL in the
description below.

